foreign  [Music]  architecturally  the king of Faso is one of many  developing nations swapping traditional  Earth brick buildings for those made of  concrete and cement while these building  materials are often seen as a sign of  development the reality is quite  different  the construction industry globally  accounts for 38 of carbon dioxide  emissions and building with concrete  makes up around eight percent of that  total  we Architects have to start listening  not just to the climate and to ecology  we seriously have to kind of own up to  the responsibility that's at stake here  I think this is the responsibility of  every future architect to think about  mitigating the damage and to look at the  proper alternatives  Clara sourdogo is one of those  Architects looking for a sustainable  alternative  she's part of a growing movement who  think reimagining how we build with mud  is the solution  often uses Banco also known as Adobe in  her buildings  the compacted sun-dried bricks make use  of whatever can be found in the local  area including soil sand clay stones and  sticks foreign  mud buildings gained a bad reputation in  Burkina Faso after there were several  reports of houses collapsing due to  extreme rainfall  as living standards have increased and  cement has become more easily accessible  people have moved away from using Earth  to build  a consecutions  sourdogo is convinced that mud can not  only withstand changes to the  environment but actually fare better  than concrete  her buildings also challenge the idea of  what mud buildings look like  um  attention  the Nubian vault is a design that was  revived by Egyptian architect Hassan  fatty in the 1940s after he saw the  technique in the Nubian Village of Abu  abrish  it has a curved structure that doesn't  require wood or metal reinforcement  making it environmentally friendly and  ideal for countries with limited natural  resources  foreign  [Music]  this year  Beyond being a recyclable and  sustainable resource Earth is also  better suited to Rising temperatures  mud has a high thermal Mass which means  it slowly absorbs and stores heat during  the day stopping a house from becoming  too hot  then when the temperature drops at night  the heat is radiated back into the room  ultimate  episode foreign  in contrast then cinder block walls with  Hollow recesses allow heat to pass  through much quicker causing the inside  of buildings to get extremely hot during  the day  many people who've built with concrete  are finding they now need to buy air  conditioning units but the cost of  installation and upkeep are too much for  some  our institutions at the moment are  adhered towards this industrial form of  computer type of generated architecture  which is devoid of any sensitivity  devoid of any interaction with nature  devoid of any interaction with the  inhabitants who are going to be living  there let alone the discussion of a  community  as Hassan Fateh used to say we're  interested in the people who have the  melaleem meaning the sense and not in  the people who have the malayin meaning  the millions you know we're talking of  The Wider population because this is  where the impact of the climate and  what's happening in the climate change  is most seen  Burkina Faso contributes just 0.08 of  global emissions but ranks as one of the  country's most vulnerable to climate  change  the landlocked Nation could see a  temperature increase of up to 1.6  degrees Celsius by 2050 and up to 4  degrees by the end of the century that's  two and a half times more than the  global average  we need a paradigm shift  and unless this happens yesterday before  today it's not going to be possible it's  not going to be possible to mitigate the  effects of the carbon footprint and the  damage the damage that we have been  creating there's this balance there's  this ecological balance about the use of  natural building materials that you  don't find anymore this is not the  architecture of the past this is the  architecture of the future  building with mud offers clear  advantages for the environment  especially for those living in hotter  climates but it's not without its  drawbacks creating structures like  sourdogos that are as sturdy as concrete  and look just as modern takes much more  time this makes it difficult to scale up  to match the demands of the modern  building sector  for now Architects are using a hybrid  method combining a small amount of  concrete into their designs which speeds  up the process but also safeguards  against more intense weather events  [Music]  foreign  conception  to phone come the the boot benefits  [Music]

